1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,260
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:05,940
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,180
My name is Catherine.

4
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:13,310
And today we've got a very interesting lesson for you all about building your vocabulary.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,880
So we're trying to describe personalities.

6
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:18,570
You're going to learn tons of new words.

7
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:19,430
Exactly.

8
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,840
And as you said, we're going to describe people's personalities.

9
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:24,960
So not their physical attributes.

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:29,630
We're going to concentrate on how they are personality wise and stuff like that.

11
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:30,920
Their character.

12
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:31,410
That's right.

13
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:37,710
So let's take a listen to today's dialogue for the first time and we'll be back in a moment to talk about some of these great words.

14
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:45,660
Okay, class, settle down.

15
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,000
I have the results of your individual personality tests.

16
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:54,560
I'm going to hand them out and if you'd like, you can read them out loud to the rest of the class.

17
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:54,840
I'll read mine.

18
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:56,260
Okay, go ahead.

19
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:00,400
It says here that I am adventurous, outgoing and easygoing.

20
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,760
It says that I'm a little superstitious and occasionally naive.

21
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:05,340
That's not true.

22
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,360
Well, the test isn't 100% accurate.

23
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:09,950
Is that all it says?

24
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:10,970
No.

25
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:16,810
It also says that I'm open minded with great ambition, but that I can also be reckless and clumsy.

26
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:18,390
This is stupid.

27
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:19,750
Okay.

28
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:21,310
Anyone else want to read theirs?

29
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:22,430
I'll go.

30
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:25,370
It says that I'm an extroverted, well balanced person.

31
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,250
It says I am generous, outspoken and very diligent.

32
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:29,790
This is so true.

33
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,210
It also says that I am a magnanimous, eloquent and daring.

34
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:34,990
This is totally me.

35
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:36,260
Whatever.

36
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:38,180
These tests are baloney.

37
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,680
All right, we're back.

38
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:48,890
So we're going to divide this up into language takeaway parts one and two.

39
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:52,170
So let's start with five key words now on language takeaway.

40
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:56,540
Language takeaway.

41
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,690
The first adjective that came up was adventurous and outgoing.

42
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,340
Outgoing, that's a good one.

43
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:06,120
Outgoing is a great one.

44
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:07,520
So think of the word out.

45
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:10,400
That means moving out, going out.

46
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:19,120
Outgoing means someone is very comfortable talking to strangers or talking to people that they don't know very well.

47
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,760
For example, my brother is very outgoing.

48
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:24,060
He loves meeting new people.

49
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:26,780
He loves starting conversations with strangers.

50
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,820
So the opposite would be shy.

51
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,480
That's the opposite.

52
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:31,060
Okay, so outgoing.

53
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:37,060
And then it also described this person as being a little superstitious.

54
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:45,750
Okay, superstitious is very interesting because it's hard to describe since everyone's superstitions are different.

55
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:48,210
In many cultures, there are different superstitions.

56
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:53,710
So for example, in America, we say that walking under a ladder is bad luck.

57
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:57,510
Or opening an umbrella inside is bad luck.

58
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:03,590
So if you believe that your actions will bring you bad luck, you're probably superstitious.

59
00:03:03,950 --> 00:03:04,990
Okay, very good.

60
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:09,840
So if you are superstitious, you believe in superstitions.

61
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:10,840
That's right.

62
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:11,840
So Marco, are you superstitious?

63
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:14,200
Do you think that black cats bring bad luck?

64
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:14,930
No, not at all.

65
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:16,200
No.

66
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,200
That's very interesting.

67
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:17,330
All right.

68
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:24,250
And this is also an interesting word, a way to describe people who are very believing, a person that is naive.

69
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:26,470
Okay, you said this already.

70
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:32,410
Naive means that someone generally believes most things that you tell them.

71
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:37,770
So if I told you, Marco, hey, did you know that it's supposed to rain frogs tomorrow?

72
00:03:38,330 --> 00:03:38,850
No way.

73
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:40,560
Really?

74
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:41,560
Yeah, really.

75
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:44,020
If Marco believes me, he's naive.

76
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:50,000
That means that probably it's not going to rain frogs tomorrow, but he believes everything I say.

77
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,800
So he's a little bit naive or inexperienced.

78
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:55,670
So naive is not really a bad way of describing a person, is it?

79
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:57,310
No, but it can be.

80
00:03:57,470 --> 00:03:58,230
So you want to be careful.

81
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:02,610
Some people, if you say that they're being naive, it's offensive.

82
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:05,210
So you have to be careful when you say this.

83
00:04:05,490 --> 00:04:05,650
Okay.

84
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:12,750
Moving on, we also had an interesting adjective that described another person, and it was to be reckless.

85
00:04:13,510 --> 00:04:14,430
I can also be reckless.

86
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:15,090
Reckless.

87
00:04:15,530 --> 00:04:15,670
Okay.

88
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:22,010
So someone who is reckless often does things without thinking, sometimes things that are dangerous.

89
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:29,680
For example, Marco, I think that driving a car without wearing your seatbelt is very reckless.

90
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:30,680
Okay.

91
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:31,850
That means that you're not thinking about safety.

92
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:33,480
Right.

93
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:39,990
So a person that is reckless is a kind of a synonym of being a little bit dangerous to be around.

94
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:40,870
That's right.

95
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:45,960
So someone who does things without thinking about their consequences, their results.

96
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:46,960
Okay.

97
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:51,110
And also along with reckless, the test said that this person was clumsy.

98
00:04:51,970 --> 00:04:52,750
Oh, I love this word.

99
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:56,510
There was a very famous commercial when I was growing up in America on the television.

100
00:04:57,350 --> 00:05:09,570
It was all about young students at high school who are clumsy, and so they should buy this special folder for their schoolwork to help them because they're so clumsy.

101
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:11,630
And they're dropping their papers and their books.

102
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:12,510
Exactly.

103
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:20,600
So someone who is clumsy often falls down or hits their head or trips or drops their things.

104
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:24,510
If I drop something on the floor and break it, my mother might say, you're so clumsy.

105
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:26,400
Clumsy fingers.

106
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:27,400
You should be more careful.

107
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,400
Yeah.

108
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,310
So clumsy, is this insulting if you say, oh, you're so clumsy?

109
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:32,830
No, it's not so bad.

110
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:36,230
There are worse things to say.

111
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,280
But if someone often drops things, you can call them clumsy.

112
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:44,040
Or you can also say, you're such a butterfingers.

113
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:45,040
Butterfingers.

114
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:46,670
That means that your fingers are like butter.

115
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:47,410
They're very slippery.

116
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,200
You drop everything.

117
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:50,200
Okay.

118
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:54,480
So clumsy and reckless, they are negative adjectives, right?

119
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,680
They're saying that you do things wrong.

120
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,590
So it's not really a good thing to be reckless or clumsy.

121
00:05:58,870 --> 00:06:00,050
No, it's not really a good thing.

122
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:02,440
These are not positive characteristics.

123
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:03,250
Okay, good.

124
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:09,870
So we took a look at those five, which are very good about one of the people that took the test.

125
00:06:10,330 --> 00:06:16,030
Let's move on now to more language and more adjectives on Language Takeaway part two.

126
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:19,730
Language Takeaway.

127
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:27,000
Alright, so in this part, we're going to take a look at positive attributes about a person, right?

128
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:28,860
They're all good things about someone.

129
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:29,440
That's right.

130
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:31,540
And this first one is very good.

131
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:34,520
Extroverted, extroverted.

132
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:36,320
A person that's extroverted.

133
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,680
So a synonym of this word is outgoing.

134
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:41,060
Okay, so it's pretty much the same thing.

135
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:44,080
That's right, because extra means going out, out.

136
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:56,100
So in this case, someone who is extroverted often meets new people, goes out and does social things, goes to the movies, goes out with friends, meets strangers, talks to strangers.

137
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:57,680
Again, it's like being outgoing.

138
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:00,640
Someone who's comfortable with new people.

139
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:02,440
And what about the opposite?

140
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:05,280
So we said that shy is the opposite of outgoing.

141
00:07:05,440 --> 00:07:08,920
What would be the opposite for extroverted?

142
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:10,840
Extroverted's opposite is introverted.

143
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:13,420
Intro means in, not out.

144
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:17,860
So that means someone who's very shy, who is uncomfortable with new people.

145
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:19,480
Okay.

146
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:24,960
And well, apart from being extroverted, the test also said that this person is very diligent.

147
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:26,920
Ooh, diligent is a great word.

148
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:30,160
This is a word you want to hear from your teachers and your bosses.

149
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:34,280
Okay, if someone says, Marco, you're very diligent at work.

150
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:34,880
What does that mean?

151
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:36,800
It means you're very careful.

152
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,400
You always do things very thoroughly.

153
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,760
And also quickly, right?

154
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,240
When you tell them to do something, they do it immediately.

155
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:45,160
That's right.

156
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:50,600
So a diligent worker always does a good job and does it in a timely manner.

157
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:51,600
Okay.

158
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:53,880
And moving on to our third adjective.

159
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,880
This one is kind of strange.

160
00:07:55,880 --> 00:08:00,220
It kind of sounds like monogamy, but it's magnanimous.

161
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:01,520
Magnanimous.

162
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:02,520
All right.

163
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:03,520
Magnanimous.

164
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:05,140
This is a great word.

165
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:11,620
We hear this a lot in the newspaper or on TV about usually famous people.

166
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:15,960
He did something very magnanimous or it was a magnanimous gesture.

167
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:21,360
This describes a person who does things for the benefit of other people.

168
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:26,920
Who is thinking about the well-being and the health and the lives of others.

169
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:31,360
So it would be a person that likes to share or a person that's not selfish.

170
00:08:31,890 --> 00:08:32,170
Right.

171
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:40,790
So if you donate half of your salary this month to victims of an earthquake, I would say, Marco, that's so magnanimous of you.

172
00:08:40,870 --> 00:08:41,850
You're so magnanimous.

173
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:43,480
Okay.

174
00:08:43,480 --> 00:08:43,700
Awesome.

175
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:51,000
And apart from being magnanimous, if a person is eloquent, I guess that's also a positive attribute.

176
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:52,720
Yeah.

177
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:53,200
This is another really good one.

178
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:56,960
Someone who is eloquent is a very good speaker.

179
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:01,500
This means that when they talk, they sound very, very good.

180
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:07,700
Someone who's eloquent uses big words, but is also very good at making other people listen.

181
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:10,010
So it's usually about speaking.

182
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:12,660
So you would say he's an eloquent speaker.

183
00:09:12,660 --> 00:09:12,810
That's right.

184
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:18,700
So someone who is eloquent is fun or good or interesting to listen to.

185
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:25,370
And our last description for personalities and the way people are is a person that's daring.

186
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:28,350
Well, this can be a good thing or a bad thing.

187
00:09:28,610 --> 00:09:30,530
It depends on who you're talking about.

188
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:35,550
Because sometimes daring people are also reckless and this can be dangerous.

189
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:39,190
But sometimes someone who is daring can be doing good things.

190
00:09:39,520 --> 00:09:44,360
Like a daring business person might take risks that other people don't take.

191
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:45,360
Okay.

192
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:49,050
So a person that's daring dares to take risks, right?

193
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:50,170
You are very daring.

194
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,570
You Marco are daring because recently you jumped out of an airplane.

195
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:55,960
Yeah.

196
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:57,040
You did some skydiving.

197
00:09:57,040 --> 00:09:58,110
So that would be considered daring.

198
00:09:58,350 --> 00:09:58,970
That's very daring.

199
00:09:59,210 --> 00:10:02,330
I'm not daring like that because I would never jump out of an airplane.

200
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:03,630
Right.

201
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:04,170
Okay.

202
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:08,860
So we took a look at basically 10 key words here.

203
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:12,620
A lot of adjectives, but they're a great way to describe yourself.

204
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:20,240
Maybe if you're at a job interview, sometimes a typical question is, how would you describe yourself using three adjectives?

205
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:23,520
I'm extroverted, I'm diligent, and I'm open-minded.

206
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:24,520
Yeah.

207
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:25,020
That's awesome.

208
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:25,260
See?

209
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:27,520
You sound really professional when you say things like this.

210
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:27,980
Give me the job.

211
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:29,520
Yeah.

212
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:30,520
Exactly.

213
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:39,160
So and even if you took a look at some of the negative adjectives that we used, it's also a typical job interview question is like, what are your biggest faults?

214
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:44,220
So you can say, well, maybe I am superstitious and naive.

215
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:46,840
You wouldn't want to say that in a job interview.

216
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:49,740
But I mean, I don't know, maybe.

217
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:51,500
Because you have to say something, right?

218
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:51,780
You do.

219
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:57,940
But I would say, sometimes I'm a bit too daring or sometimes I take things personally.

220
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,760
So we can talk about that another time.

221
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:07,860
But I think for now, it'd be good if we listened to today's dialogue one more time and really got a good sense of how to use these words.

222
00:11:14,910 --> 00:11:16,190
Okay, class, settle down.

223
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:19,520
I have the results of your individual personality tests.

224
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:21,760
I am going to hand them out.

225
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:24,360
And if you'd like, you can read them out loud to the rest of the class.

226
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:25,380
I'll read mine.

227
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:26,800
Okay, go ahead.

228
00:11:27,540 --> 00:11:30,940
It says here that I am adventurous, outgoing, and easygoing.

229
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:34,340
It says that I'm a little superstitious and occasionally naive.

230
00:11:35,220 --> 00:11:35,880
That's not true.

231
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:38,900
Well, the test isn't 100% accurate.

232
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:40,500
Is that all it says?

233
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:41,520
No.

234
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:47,340
It also says that I'm open-minded with great ambition, but that I can also be reckless and clumsy.

235
00:11:48,380 --> 00:11:48,960
This is stupid.

236
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:52,640
Okay, anyone else want to read theirs?

237
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:52,960
I'll go.

238
00:11:53,640 --> 00:11:55,920
It says that I'm an extroverted, well-balanced person.

239
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:58,800
It says I am generous, outspoken, and very diligent.

240
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:00,320
This is so true.

241
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:03,760
It also says that I am a magnanimous, eloquent, and daring.

242
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:05,520
This is totally me.

243
00:12:06,290 --> 00:12:06,810
Whatever.

244
00:12:07,570 --> 00:12:08,710
These tests are baloney.

245
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:16,650
Alright, we're back.

246
00:12:16,990 --> 00:12:20,130
So now going into descriptions.

247
00:12:21,130 --> 00:12:26,110
What are three adjectives that you would use to describe someone that you hate the most?

248
00:12:26,890 --> 00:12:34,770
So going back to describing people, how would you describe one of the main things that really irritate you about someone?

249
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:39,770
Maybe if they are clumsy, it really bothers you that people are clumsy or reckless maybe.

250
00:12:41,010 --> 00:12:41,530
I see what you're saying.

251
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:51,540
Well, I really like extroverted, hardworking people, but in English we have a word to describe someone who is too extroverted, too hardworking.

252
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:53,510
We call this person a type A personality.

253
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:55,770
Type A. Type A.

254
00:12:55,910 --> 00:13:04,370
The letter A. And this means someone who works very, very hard, who is very, very serious, but who's always anxious, always needs to get things done.

255
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:13,040
And so these people can be very hard to work with because they're controlling and they're never calm.

256
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:14,040
So they're not easygoing.

257
00:13:14,510 --> 00:13:15,690
No, they're not easygoing.

258
00:13:16,110 --> 00:13:18,350
They always want to do what they want to do.

259
00:13:18,390 --> 00:13:19,830
They're usually not very good listeners.

260
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:33,320
And so this type A personality is something that I would say I'm not a big fan of at work or even when we're going out because with friends, it's hard to be around someone who always has to control you.

261
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:34,200
This is very interesting.

262
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:43,320
And I'm sure our listeners have a lot of different ways to describe things that irritate them, maybe about people or maybe attributes that they really admire about someone.

263
00:13:44,040 --> 00:13:44,160
That's right.

264
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:47,000
And I want to give everybody a little bit of homework today.

265
00:13:47,520 --> 00:13:54,890
Go home or if you're at home, look up a word in the dictionary that describes yourself.

266
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:56,160
Yeah.

267
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:58,080
An adjective to describe yourself.

268
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:04,520
I think this is a really good homework assignment because you'll be improving your vocabulary and then put it on our website, EnglishPod.com.

269
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:05,830
Let us know more about you.

270
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:06,230
Okay.

271
00:14:06,390 --> 00:14:07,130
We'll see everyone there.

272
00:14:07,430 --> 00:14:07,670
Bye everyone.
